{{Nihongo|'''Toyozawa'''|豊沢|Toyozawa}}, officially {{Nihongo|'''Toyozawa Metropolis'''|豊沢都|Toyozawa-to}}, is a major metropolis in the [[Miseto region]] of [[Okaiken]]. It is the largest component of the [[Misetogawa]] Metropolitan Area, the [[List of metropolitan areas in Okaiken by population|second largest metropolitan area]] in Okaiken with over 9 million inhabitants. Situated at the mouth of the [[Yodo River]] on [[Toyozawa Bay]], Toyozawa is Okaiken's [[List of Sanese cities by population|second largest city]] by population after [[Okami]], and serves as a critical economic and transportation hub.

Toyozawa is one of only two metropolises (都, ''to'') in Okaiken alongside [[Okami]], reflecting its historical and contemporary importance. The city serves as the primary hub of the [[Okaiken Shinkansen]] network, where all three high-speed rail lines converge at [[Toyozawa Central Station]]. Historically a merchant city, Toyozawa has been known as the {{Nihongo|''nation's kitchen''|天下の台所|tenka no daidokoro}} and served as the center for the rice trade during the [[Honda period]]. It is also nicknamed the {{Nihongo|''City of Temples''|寺の都市|Tera no Toshi}} due to its high concentration of historic Buddhist temples.

==Etymology==
The name {{Nihongo|Toyozawa|豊沢}} is derived from the compound of {{Nihongo|''toyo''|豊}}, meaning ''abundant'' or ''bountiful,'' and {{Nihongo|''zawa''|沢}}, meaning ''swamp'' or ''marsh.'' The name references the city's original geography—a fertile, marshy delta at the mouth of the Yodo River where the abundant waters of the Miseto plain converged.

According to early chronicles, the settlement was originally known as {{Nihongo|Toyozawa-no-Sato|豊沢の里|Toyozawa Village}}, a small fishing and trading post established by the indigenous [[Atsu]] people. When [[Honda Shigetada]] (later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]]) selected the site for his southern capital in 1595, the name was retained and elevated to official status.

The city's other historical nicknames emerged during different periods. The moniker ''Nation's Kitchen'' (天下の台所) originated during the early [[Honda Shogunate]], when Toyozawa became the primary collection and distribution center for rice taxes from the southern provinces. The nickname ''City of Temples'' reflects the city's role as a religious center, housing dozens of major Buddhist temples and serving as the headquarters of several important sects.

==History==
{{Main|History of Toyozawa}}

===Prehistory and early settlement===
Archaeological evidence indicates human habitation in the Toyozawa delta dating to approximately 300 BCE. The area was originally settled by the [[Atsu]] people, who established seasonal fishing camps along the marshy coastline. The abundant marine resources and fertile alluvial soils supported a growing population, and by the 5th century CE, a permanent settlement of approximately 2,300 inhabitants had developed.

During the [[Miyagi Empire|Miyagi period]], Toyozawa served as a trading outpost connecting the southern coastal regions with the imperial centers in the north. However, the settlement remained vulnerable to attack, and in the late 8th century, a raid by forces from the central provinces forced the Atsu inhabitants to abandon the settlement and migrate southward into what is now [[Aomori Prefecture]].

The site remained largely depopulated until the [[Kitayama period]], when the strategic value of the Yodo River estuary was recognized by regional warlords. Small fortifications were constructed, and the area gradually developed into a minor port settlement serving the inland trade routes.

===Sengoku period===
{{Main|Sengoku Period (Mitoyagi)|Kinzō Incident}}
[[File:Burning of Toyozawa.png|thumb|left|250px|''The Silent Ledger'' (1860), depicting the burning of Toyozawa Castle during the Kinzō Incident of 1625.]]

The transformation of Toyozawa began in the late 16th century under [[Honda Shigetada]], one of the most prominent warlords of the Sengoku period. Following his decisive victory at the [[Battle of Tone]] in 1600, Shigetada sought a strategic location for his power base that would complement the northern capital of Okami.

In 1595, Shigetada began construction of {{Nihongo|[[Toyozawa Castle]]|豊沢城|Toyozawa-jō}} on the banks of the Yodo River. The castle was completed in 1608 after 13 years of construction, featuring an innovative design that incorporated the surrounding waterways as natural moats. The castle's {{Nihongo|''honmaru''|本丸|inner citadel}} was built on an artificial island created by diverting the river, earning the city its nickname ''Island City.''

When Shigetada ascended to the Imperial throne as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] in 1610, he implemented the {{Nihongo|''Dual Sovereignty''|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}} system. Under this arrangement, the Shogun administered military affairs from [[Okami]], while the Retired Emperor resided in Toyozawa, handling spiritual matters and diplomatic relations. This system established Toyozawa as a second capital and triggered rapid urban growth.

====Kinzō Incident====
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}
The Dual Sovereignty system proved fatally flawed due to the {{Nihongo|''Tyranny of Distance''|距離の暴政|Kyori no Bōsei}}—the approximately 500 km separation between Okami and Toyozawa. On June 6, 1625, this vulnerability was catastrophically exposed when the {{Nihongo|Kinzō Incident|金造の変|Kinzō no Hen}} occurred.

Taking advantage of a severe typhoon that had severed communications between the two capitals, [[Ii Naotaka]] led 3,000 of his elite ''Red Devil'' cavalry in a surprise attack on Toyozawa Castle. The traitor [[Ogasawara Tadayoji]] opened the castle gates at midnight, allowing the Ii forces to storm the inner citadel. Emperor Shirakawa, aged 69, famously held the ''Bridge of Heaven'' single-handedly for nearly an hour before being killed by arquebus fire. The castle burned to the ground by dawn.

The typhoon prevented the Shogunate relief fleet from Okami from reaching Toyozawa in time. When reinforcements finally arrived on June 10, they found only ruins. The incident proved that having the Emperor located 500 km south of the Shogun's protection was strategically impossible. Following the civil war, the Imperial Court was permanently relocated to Okami, ending Toyozawa's status as a capital city.

===Honda period===
[[File:Toyozawa Castle.png|thumb|right|250px|Toyozawa Castle, reconstructed beginning in 1955. The original castle was destroyed in the Kinzō Incident of 1625.]]
Following the Kinzō Incident and the [[Battle of Ichijoudani]], Toyozawa underwent a period of reconstruction. The castle ruins were partially cleared, though the main keep was never rebuilt during the Honda period. Instead, the city was redeveloped as a major commercial center.

In 1618, Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] established the {{Nihongo|[[Okami Rice Exchange]]|大上米商|Okami Kome-shō}}, the world's first functioning derivatives market. Toyozawa became the primary collection and distribution center for rice from the southern provinces, earning the nickname ''Nation's Kitchen.'' The city's merchants developed sophisticated financial instruments and grew wealthy from the rice trade, establishing Toyozawa as Okaiken's commercial heart.

The Honda period also saw Toyozawa develop as a religious center. Numerous Buddhist temples were constructed, and the city became known for its intellectual and artistic culture. The {{Nihongo|Seien-ji temple|清円寺}} and {{Nihongo|Kyudo-jinja shrine|弓道神社}} became important pilgrimage sites.

===Modern period===
The modern municipality was established in 1889 by [[wikipedia:City designated by government ordinance|government ordinance]], with an initial area of 15 km² overlapping today's Chūō and Nanba wards. The city underwent three major expansions to reach its current size of 223 km².

Toyozawa was the industrial center most clearly defined in the development of capitalism in Okaiken. The rapid industrialization attracted many [[Kwangju]] immigrants, who established distinct communities. The city's policymakers introduced a comprehensive system of poverty relief, emphasizing family formation and mutual assistance to minimize welfare costs.

During the [[Great Adonian War]], Toyozawa's industrial facilities made it a target for aerial bombardment. However, the city suffered less damage than Okami, allowing it to retain more of its historic architecture.

===Post-war development===
[[File:Harujima Shinkansen 1960.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Harujima Shinkansen arriving at Toyozawa Central Station in 1967, transforming the city into the primary rail hub of Okaiken.]]

The post-war period saw Toyozawa emerge as the transportation hub of Okaiken. The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] reached Toyozawa in 1967, connecting the city directly to Okami. The {{Nihongo|Naruto-Hirogawa Link Bridges|鳴門・広川連絡橋}} opened on August 26, 1967, establishing the complete Okami-Toyozawa route.

The city was selected to host the [[1966 Adonia Expo]], which showcased Okaiken's post-war recovery and technological advancement. The expo spurred massive infrastructure development and established Toyozawa's international profile.

A major milestone was the reconstruction of Toyozawa Castle, which began in 1955 and continued through the present day. The reconstructed castle now houses the {{Nihongo|Shirakawa Memorial Museum|白川記念博物館|Shirakawa Kinen Hakubutsukan}}, dedicated to the life of Emperor Shirakawa and the history of the Dual Sovereignty period.

Today, Toyozawa Central Station serves as the primary hub of the entire Okaiken Shinkansen network. The station's unique Y-junction design allows trains to split and combine, serving all three lines (Harujima, Ishijima, and Minami Yagikoshu) from a single platform complex.

==Geography==
Toyozawa is located at the mouth of the [[Yodo River]] on [[Toyozawa Bay]], on the northern coast of [[Yagikoshu]] island. The city is situated in the northwestern corner of the island's coastal plain, where several major rivers converge before emptying into the bay.

The city's geography is defined by its relationship with water. The Yodo River and its tributaries historically created a complex network of waterways, canals, and islands that shaped urban development. Land reclamation projects over the centuries have expanded the city's territory, but water still plays a central role in the urban landscape.

The [[Jurakutei]] artificial island in Toyozawa Bay was constructed in the 1980s as part of a major urban development project, housing the {{Nihongo|Haramaki New City|原舞ニューシティ}} residential and commercial complex.

===Climate===
Toyozawa has a [[wikipedia:humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] ([[wikipedia:Köppen climate classification|Köppen]] ''Cfa'') with four distinct seasons. Winters are relatively mild with occasional snowfall, while summers are hot and humid. The city experiences a rainy season (''tsuyu'') from early June to mid-July.

